[{:db/id 17592186175991 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63d21bd1-32a2-5018-8996-67c94ddd1ddb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175958 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cf83320f-aed3-5360-8d91-4014e5072dc4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-63d21bd1-32a2-5018-8996-67c94ddd1ddb" :question/id #uuid "63d21bd1-32a2-5018-8996-67c94ddd1ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-126779" :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{11}, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{11}, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{11}, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{11}+(-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{11}+{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{11} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{11}+1\\cdot \\frac{5}{11}+0\\cdot \\frac{4}{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3108c7f3-de2f-5d87-917e-86599d83d83e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3108c7f3-de2f-5d87-917e-86599d83d83e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3108c7f3-de2f-5d87-917e-86599d83d83e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-39031138-657e-5f4b-9c5a-5ffdc8c22c84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0aaacd1-dbaa-53a1-b38b-5a10a1d2faee" "answer-ad40040f-3c97-59ee-99db-2b9157068b63" "answer-39031138-657e-5f4b-9c5a-5ffdc8c22c84" "answer-880594b0-02c0-573e-b57f-4ba4d8c79d1a" "answer-27659c3a-1630-525f-9403-71b378299d16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0aaacd1-dbaa-53a1-b38b-5a10a1d2faee" :answer/id #uuid "e0aaacd1-dbaa-53a1-b38b-5a10a1d2faee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad40040f-3c97-59ee-99db-2b9157068b63" :answer/id #uuid "ad40040f-3c97-59ee-99db-2b9157068b63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39031138-657e-5f4b-9c5a-5ffdc8c22c84" :answer/id #uuid "39031138-657e-5f4b-9c5a-5ffdc8c22c84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-880594b0-02c0-573e-b57f-4ba4d8c79d1a" :answer/id #uuid "880594b0-02c0-573e-b57f-4ba4d8c79d1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27659c3a-1630-525f-9403-71b378299d16" :answer/id #uuid "27659c3a-1630-525f-9403-71b378299d16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf83320f-aed3-5360-8d91-4014e5072dc4" :question/id #uuid "cf83320f-aed3-5360-8d91-4014e5072dc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110007" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[(2Y-5)^{2}]$ if $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=30$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding out the square inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(2Y-5)^{2}] & =\\text{E}[4{Y}^{2}-20Y+25] \\\\  & =4\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-20\\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[25] \\\\  & =4\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-20\\text{E}[Y]+25 \\\\  & =4(30)-20(5)+25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fec6f475-22fd-5925-b6c9-29a82704ff35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fec6f475-22fd-5925-b6c9-29a82704ff35" :answer-field/id #uuid "fec6f475-22fd-5925-b6c9-29a82704ff35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db161ce8-8a10-5a17-9d20-1b084deeabcf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4209d557-5cad-5013-a9c1-65ea6323cb85" "answer-d9824405-03bd-5272-8234-ee03e3aa2f5b" "answer-da0f3a81-b8de-5653-9e39-6724d4505444" "answer-db161ce8-8a10-5a17-9d20-1b084deeabcf" "answer-1bc64c55-cc10-5bf4-80a0-019a0f33bb00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4209d557-5cad-5013-a9c1-65ea6323cb85" :answer/id #uuid "4209d557-5cad-5013-a9c1-65ea6323cb85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9824405-03bd-5272-8234-ee03e3aa2f5b" :answer/id #uuid "d9824405-03bd-5272-8234-ee03e3aa2f5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da0f3a81-b8de-5653-9e39-6724d4505444" :answer/id #uuid "da0f3a81-b8de-5653-9e39-6724d4505444" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db161ce8-8a10-5a17-9d20-1b084deeabcf" :answer/id #uuid "db161ce8-8a10-5a17-9d20-1b084deeabcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bc64c55-cc10-5bf4-80a0-019a0f33bb00" :answer/id #uuid "1bc64c55-cc10-5bf4-80a0-019a0f33bb00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "47" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177004 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a discrete random variable $X$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of $X,$ denoted $\\textrm E[X],$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability mass function of $X.$ \n\nTherefore, we can calculate the expected value of the random variable $X^2$ as\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIn this case, $\\textrm E[X^2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+9\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing $\\textrm E[X^2],$ it's important to remember that we still evaluate the probability mass function as $f(x),$ *not* $f(x^2).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177003 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment**, respectively. \n\nIt's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm E[X^3]$ is the **third moment. **\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X] =  \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)$ is the first moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ is the second moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^3] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^3 \\cdot f(x)$ is the third moment\n\n- $\\,\\cdots$\n\nIn general, the $n$th  moment is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^n] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^n \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nThe concept of a moment will become important later. In particular, we'll soon see how $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ can be used to compute the **variance** of a random variable.\n\nFinally, note that $\\textrm E[X], \\textrm E[X^2], \\textrm E[X^3],$ etc. are sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moment. \n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176159 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
